#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paints the far level of Navigation's instance maps, the dungeons and raids (Doc/guides/map-style.md;
Doc/ideas/Navigation.md, *Instance maps as the world map, apart from it*): one realistic reimagination of each
instance as a whole, seen at an angle, by the local image model in ComfyUI, strictly from its brief
(dev/imagegen/maps/dungeons.toml), the words the world map's painted levels share (levels.toml, [far]) and two
pictures, and checked after it is painted.

  image 1   the instance's layout: its floors where they lie (each floor's place in the world, Core's set
            Dungeons), seen from high above at an angle (TILT), a floor that lies over another lifted above it
            (LIFT); parts far apart (the wings of a monastery) moved together in their own directions, so the
            instance is one compact place, never a row of panels. Drawn from the game's map art of the floors (its
            frame and title banner cut off, the paper around the rooms left out), stronger, blurred to shapes (BLUR):
            no line, drawing style or writing carries over. An instance the game has no map art of takes the
            pictures given in --layouts (<map ID>-<floor>.png), stacked.
  image 2   what the place looks like in the game: the game's own painting of it (the dungeon journal's, else its
            loading screen; --pictures, the game's files as <map ID>-lore.png and <map ID>-loading.png), cut free
            of frame and logo. Its architecture, materials, colours, light and mood are taken, never its view.
  check     each painting is looked at by the vision model the image model reads its words with (Qwen3-VL,
            workflow qwen3vl-look): writing in it, a map or a drawing instead of a place, separate panels, and
            whether it is seen from above as a whole (not image 2's view copied), and whether it is the kind of
            place the brief says (biome). A painting that fails is kept, marked, and the
            next seed is painted, until SEEDS paintings pass or TRIES were painted.

The middle and close levels of the instances, once made, take the place of the map art in image 1 and join as a
third picture (Saneroki, 2026-10-21: the far level follows the layout and takes them as inspiration); until then
the queue's job waits. The first try of every instance comes before the second of any, so a run that stops early
has some of each; a painting already made is not made again (the GPU runner can stop and start it). A run paints
with the plan it began with (its copy in plan/, as the Atlas keeps one): a change shows in its status and takes
effect with rebuild.

Usage:
  dev/tools/dungeon-paint paint [--mapart DIR] [--layouts DIR] [--pictures DIR] [--only KEYS] [--limit N]
                                [--out DIR] [--dry]
  dev/tools/dungeon-paint check [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/dungeon-paint status [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/dungeon-paint contact [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/dungeon-paint rebuild [--out DIR]

  paint      paints what is missing; --mapart, --layouts and --pictures are needed the first time, then remembered
             in the run's state.json; --only 33,36 those instances only; --limit N stops after N paintings; --dry
             makes the inputs and prints the words, without ComfyUI
  check      looks again at every painting not looked at yet (one made before the check, or by another run)
  status     its first line: "<made> of <all> paintings made"; then what has no map, no picture or no brief, the
             paintings that failed their check, and whether the plan changed since the run began
  contact    contact-far.jpg: a row per instance, its two pictures and its paintings (a red bar on one that failed)
  rebuild    the run so far put aside whole in <out>-runs/<date>, a new run with the plans as they are

It writes, in the run's folder (default assets/generated/dungeons, never committed):
  input/far-<key>.png, input/ref-<key>.png     images 1 and 2 as the model gets them
  far/<key>-<seed>.png                         the paintings
  checks.json                                  per painting what the vision model saw, and whether it passed
  paintings.jsonl                              a line per painting: key, seed, words, workflow, settings, time
  plan/                                        the plan the run paints with: dungeons.toml, levels.toml, workflows
  state.json                                   the paths given, when the run began
Needs Python with Pillow and NumPy, and ComfyUI (comfyui start; the GPU runner starts it for its job).
"""
